WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.370 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A family is planning a trip to the
wildlife park.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.990 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Three buses leave at different times.

3
00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.790 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Which one arrives before the gates open?

4
00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:14.702 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Today we are learning to read timetables
and calculate travel durations.

5
00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:20.469 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Our learning goal is to read timetables
and calculate travel durations.

6
00:00:21.462 --> 00:00:26.222 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Success criteria: read departure times,
calculate elapsed time and

7
00:00:26.222 --> 00:00:27.702 line:1% position:50% size:90%
choose a suitable journey.

8
00:00:28.706 --> 00:00:33.636 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Read the departure and arrival times from
the same service on the timetable.

9
00:00:33.736 --> 00:00:36.686 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Different rows can refer to different
journeys.

10
00:00:37.686 --> 00:00:40.136 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A timeline makes the journey visible.

11
00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:45.086 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Begin at eight twenty-five, move to nine
o'clock and then move to nine ten.

12
00:00:46.114 --> 00:00:50.134 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How many minutes pass between eight
twenty-five and nine ten?

13
00:00:50.164 --> 00:00:52.554 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Estimate first, then calculate.

14
00:00:57.603 --> 00:01:02.363 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Thirty-five minutes to nine o'clock plus
ten minutes to nine ten makes forty-

15
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:03.403 line:1% position:50% size:90%
five minutes.

16
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:09.060 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Twenty-four-hour time removes confusion
between morning and afternoon.

17
00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.680 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Thirteen fifteen means one fifteen in the
afternoon.

18
00:01:13.713 --> 00:01:18.433 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A train departs at thirteen fifteen and
arrives at fourteen oh five.

19
00:01:18.463 --> 00:01:20.423 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How long is the journey?

20
00:01:25.416 --> 00:01:29.286 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Forty-five minutes to fourteen hundred,
then five more minutes.

21
00:01:29.336 --> 00:01:31.546 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The journey lasts fifty minutes.

22
00:01:32.594 --> 00:01:37.244 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Subtracting the minute numbers alone is a
mistake when you cross an hour.

23
00:01:37.274 --> 00:01:39.594 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Use the full timeline instead.

24
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:45.117 line:1% position:50% size:90%
For a deadline, consider arrival time, not
just departure time.

25
00:01:45.227 --> 00:01:48.597 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Arriving after an event begins may be too
late.

26
00:01:49.593 --> 00:01:51.833 line:1% position:50% size:90%
You must arrive before ten.

27
00:01:51.893 --> 00:01:54.913 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A service arrives at nine thirty-five.

28
00:01:54.953 --> 00:01:56.763 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Does it meet the deadline?

29
00:02:01.761 --> 00:02:06.321 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Yes. Nine thirty-five is twenty-five
minutes before ten, so

30
00:02:06.321 --> 00:02:08.171 line:1% position:50% size:90%
the service meets the deadline.

31
00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:12.534 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Remember to include walking and waiting
time.

32
00:02:12.584 --> 00:02:16.484 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Reaching the station exactly when the bus
leaves is risky.

33
00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:21.579 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A bus departs at nine twenty and arrives
at ten oh five.

34
00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:23.780 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How many minutes does the trip take?

35
00:02:28.797 --> 00:02:32.247 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Forty minutes to ten o'clock, then five
more minutes.

36
00:02:32.287 --> 00:02:34.767 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The trip lasts forty-five minutes.

37
00:02:35.802 --> 00:02:38.072 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Check that your answer is sensible.

38
00:02:38.222 --> 00:02:42.062 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A journey crossing an hour can be longer
than the difference between the

39
00:02:42.062 --> 00:02:43.222 line:1% position:50% size:90%
minute digits.

40
00:02:44.221 --> 00:02:48.561 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A train leaves at eleven thirty-five and
arrives at twelve twenty.

41
00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:51.371 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Calculate the duration using noon.

42
00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.630 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Twenty-five minutes to noon plus twenty
minutes afterwards makes forty-five

43
00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:01.310 line:1% position:50% size:90%
minutes.

44
00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.880 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Read the correct timetable entry, draw a
timeline, cross the hour boundary

45
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.350 line:1% position:50% size:90%
and add the intervals.

46
00:03:09.345 --> 00:03:11.595 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Think about a school bus timetable.

47
00:03:11.715 --> 00:03:15.925 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The departure time tells us when to be at
the stop, while the arrival time

48
00:03:15.925 --> 00:03:18.565 line:1% position:50% size:90%
tells us when we can reach our
destination.

49
00:03:19.579 --> 00:03:23.599 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If the bus is delayed, our original plan
may no longer work.

50
00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:27.749 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A good traveller checks whether there is
enough time before an important

51
00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:28.619 line:1% position:50% size:90%
appointment.

52
00:03:29.646 --> 00:03:31.616 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Try a different strategy.

53
00:03:31.646 --> 00:03:35.646 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Instead of counting every minute, split
the journey at the nearest whole

54
00:03:35.646 --> 00:03:37.866 line:1% position:50% size:90%
hour and add the two intervals.

55
00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:43.630 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A journey from nine thirty-five to ten
o'clock takes twenty-five minutes.

56
00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:47.890 line:1% position:50% size:90%
We know this because sixty minus
thirty-five equals twenty-five.

57
00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:51.514 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Check your answer by counting forward.

58
00:03:51.544 --> 00:03:55.794 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Start at nine thirty-five, add twenty-five
minutes, and you should reach ten

59
00:03:55.794 --> 00:03:56.574 line:1% position:50% size:90%
o'clock.

60
00:03:57.574 --> 00:04:01.854 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If you have to change buses, calculate the
waiting time between the first

61
00:04:01.854 --> 00:04:03.894 line:1% position:50% size:90%
arrival and the second departure.

62
00:04:03.914 --> 00:04:06.444 line:1% position:50% size:90%
That wait is part of the whole trip.

63
00:04:07.512 --> 00:04:11.882 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A timetable is a promise about planned
times, but it is not the same as a

64
00:04:11.882 --> 00:04:16.442 line:1% position:50% size:90%
clock. We use the timetable to predict
when the journey should happen.

65
00:04:17.448 --> 00:04:22.158 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Suppose you need ten minutes to walk from
the bus stop to the wildlife park.

66
00:04:22.158 --> 00:04:27.058 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A bus arriving at nine fifty-five would
not get you through the gate by ten.

67
00:04:28.089 --> 00:04:32.329 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When comparing two routes, the shortest
ride is not always the earliest

68
00:04:32.329 --> 00:04:36.739 line:1% position:50% size:90%
arrival. A later departure may still
arrive sooner if it travels more

69
00:04:36.739 --> 00:04:37.539 line:1% position:50% size:90%
directly.

70
00:04:38.552 --> 00:04:43.052 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a service that leaves at eight
forty and arrives at nine twenty.

71
00:04:43.052 --> 00:04:46.762 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Count twenty minutes to nine, then twenty
more to nine twenty.

72
00:04:47.775 --> 00:04:51.595 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A transfer means leaving one service and
boarding another.

73
00:04:51.615 --> 00:04:56.055 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Calculate the gap between the first
arrival and the second departure before

74
00:04:56.055 --> 00:04:57.265 line:1% position:50% size:90%
choosing the route.

75
00:04:58.282 --> 00:05:02.552 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If your connection arrives at nine ten and
the next bus leaves at nine

76
00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:05.292 line:1% position:50% size:90%
twelve, you have only two minutes to
change.

77
00:05:05.322 --> 00:05:07.352 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Is that a sensible plan?

78
00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:12.305 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The answer depends on how far apart the
platforms are.

79
00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:16.655 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Good planning includes a safety margin,
not only the arithmetic.

80
00:05:17.664 --> 00:05:21.694 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Now create a small timetable with two
departure options.

81
00:05:21.714 --> 00:05:26.294 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Give each an arrival time and decide which
service is better for a morning

82
00:05:26.294 --> 00:05:27.144 line:1% position:50% size:90%
appointment.

83
00:05:28.185 --> 00:05:30.415 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Estimate before you calculate.

84
00:05:30.445 --> 00:05:34.805 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A journey from eleven thirty-five to
twelve twenty must be shorter than an

85
00:05:34.805 --> 00:05:38.225 line:1% position:50% size:90%
hour, because twelve thirty-five would be
one hour later.

86
00:05:39.262 --> 00:05:43.172 line:1% position:50% size:90%
You can explain your thinking with words,
numbers or a timeline.

87
00:05:43.192 --> 00:05:46.802 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The strongest answer shows where each
interval comes from.
